<h2 id="sec-1">The Verified Weekly Price</h2>
<p>The verified weekly rate for the electric motorbike in Hanoi is 1,000,000 VND for a full week on the same machine. That figure comes straight from the shop's published prices, and this guide adds nothing invented to it: no promotions, no bundles, no estimates. The number on the price list is the planning number.</p>
<p>Divided across seven days, the week averages roughly 143,000 VND per day, against 200,000 VND per day at the daily rate. The saving is 400,000 VND over a full week paid daily, which is why the weekly rate is the default structure for any stay touching five days or more.</p>
<p>The price sits in the step-up tier. The Honda Vision and Honda Air Blade share the same verified weekly figure of 1,000,000 VND, so choosing inside the tier is a choice of machine, not of price. The entry tier, Honda Wave, Yamaha Sirius, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click, rents at 700,000 VND per week.</p>
<p>A weekly rental is also a commitment decision. The week books one machine for seven days, so the plan should be honest about how many of those days the electric will actually roll. A week with four riding days still beats four daily rates, but only barely, and that math belongs before the counter, not after.</p>
<h2 id="sec-2">What the Electric Buys for a Week</h2>
<p>The electric motorbike is the quiet choice of the fleet, and a week of Hanoi riding is where the quiet pays daily. Silent running changes the character of early Old Quarter mornings and evening lake loops, instant gentle delivery makes the constant stop-start of city traffic almost relaxing, and the absence of fuel stops removes an entire errand from the week.</p>
<p>Simplicity matters most over a week, because a week is long enough for fatigue. Seven days of stop-and-go is easiest on a machine with no clutch, no shift timing and no petrol station stops, and riders new to Vietnam consistently find the electric the least demanding option in the fleet to live with.</p>
<p>The honest summary is that the electric is the choice for riders who want the calmest, simplest possible week: no noise, no gears, no fuel line in the budget at all. For a week of short city loops at the step-up price, it is the correct answer, and the charging question is one conversation at handover rather than a daily stop.</p>
<h2 id="sec-3">Planning a Weekly Budget</h2>
<p>Plan the week from three lines: the fixed 1,000,000 VND rental, the charging arrangement instead of a fuel line, and the small daily costs of riding, paid parking at a few thousand VND per stop where lots are used. Charging on a city week costs close to nothing, which makes an electric weekly budget the flattest in the fleet.</p>
<p>Set the week around a realistic rhythm. A typical tourist week is Old Quarter evenings, a Hoan Kiem loop, a Temple of Literature and West Lake day, a market run, and one trip across Long Bien bridge for the views. Each ride is short, and the electric carries a week of them inside its daily charges.</p>
<p>Keep a day un-ridden deliberately. A rainy Hanoi afternoon happens, and on the weekly rate a parked day costs roughly 143,000 VND of unused daily value, real money on the step-up tier. This is the quiet advantage of the week over the day: the day rate punishes a rest day at full price, while the week absorbs it. Plan five or six honest riding days and let weather take the rest.</p>
<p>The budgeting mistake is planning seven full riding days. A budget with slack survives contact with a real Hanoi week, and a week with slack still beats five daily rates.</p>
<h2 id="sec-4">The Daily Rate Comparison</h2>
<p>The electric's daily rate is 200,000 VND, so five daily rates total exactly 1,000,000 VND, matching the weekly rate to the dong. The break-even sits at five days: any stay of five days or more should be booked weekly, and the counter can convert an ongoing daily rental on request. Ask, because conversion terms belong to the desk.</p>
<p>Below five days the daily rate stays honest. Two days cost 400,000 VND, three cost 600,000 VND, four cost 800,000 VND, all under the week. The two structures are tools for different plans, and the honest planner matches the structure to the calendar.</p>
<p>The comparison mistake is extending daily past the fifth day. Riders do it out of inertia, and the arithmetic quietly costs 200,000 VND per day. Mark the fifth day as decision day and the week takes over at exactly the right moment.</p>
<h2 id="sec-5">The Monthly Rate Comparison</h2>
<p>The electric's monthly rate is a verified 1,500,000 VND per month. Even two weeks booked weekly total 2,000,000 VND, a third more than the entire month, so roughly ten to eleven days of weekly rental already approach the monthly figure. Any stay touching ten days should ask about the monthly rate before paying for a second week, and the counter will run the exact dates in a minute.</p>
<p>The monthly saving is dramatic. Four weeks booked back to back total 4,000,000 VND against a month at 1,500,000 VND, and even two weeks cost more than the whole month. From about ten days on, the month is not just cheaper, it is cheap in absolute terms: a silent month of city transport for 1,500,000 VND.</p>
<p>The honest ladder is: days below five pay daily, five to about ten pays weekly, ten days and beyond confirms the 1,500,000 VND monthly rate. The <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub puts every model against every period in one place.</p>
<h2 id="sec-6">Electric Versus Other Weekly Rates</h2>
<p>Within the step-up tier the weekly price is identical: the electric motorbike, Honda Vision and Honda Air Blade all rent at 1,000,000 VND per week. The electric is the silence and simplicity choice, the Vision is the storage and comfort choice, and the Air Blade is the performance choice. Price does not vote inside the tier; the riding week does.</p>
<p>The entry tier costs 300,000 VND less per week. The Honda Wave and Yamaha Sirius are semi-automatic underbones with a foot shifter, while the Yamaha Mio and Honda Click are twist-and-go automatics, all at 700,000 VND per week. The step-up premium buys silent, gearless electric running, and riders who do not care about that should save the 300,000 VND.</p>
<p>There is also the 50cc option for riders who want the smallest petrol engine class, at a verified 200,000 VND per day. For weekly terms on a 50cc machine, the desk confirms the figure for your dates, which is how a verified-prices shop works.</p>
<p>The comparison mistake is upgrading without a reason. A week of short Old Quarter hops does not need electric silence, and 300,000 VND buys several very good meals in Hanoi. Upgrade for the riding you actually do, not for the brochure in your head.</p>
<h2 id="sec-7">Deposits, Gear and Extras</h2>
<p>The weekly rate is the headline, not the whole transaction. Ask what the deposit is, in what form it is held, and what has to be true at return for it to come back in full. An electric machine may carry a different deposit conversation than a petrol one, so ask rather than assume. Cash deposits get receipts and card holds get release dates, and both questions take ten seconds each.</p>
<p>Ask what is included with the week: helmets for each rider, a lock, rain ponchos. A week is long enough for Hanoi rain, and included gear beats improvised gear every time. Inclusion is a question, never an assumption, and confirming at handover prevents a mid-week surprise.</p>
<p>Ask the electric question before it matters: how the machine charges, where, what a day of city riding does to the battery, and what the desk expects at return. A weekly rental is a relationship, and the charging answer should be settled on day one, not improvised on day four.</p>
<p>The extras mistake is signing in a hurry. The weekly agreement is one page and takes two minutes to read, and every verbal answer about deposit, charging and inclusions becomes a promise on that page. Read it in the shade before riding off, and the week starts with nothing unresolved.</p>
<h2 id="sec-8">When the Week Is the Wrong Rate</h2>
<p>The week is the wrong structure for stays of four days or fewer, where the daily rate totals less than 1,000,000 VND, and for stays of ten days or more, where the verified 1,500,000 VND monthly rate should be taken instead. Between those boundaries, five to nine days, the week is exactly right, and the roughly 143,000 VND daily average is the cheapest verified way to ride the electric short of a month.</p>
<p>It is also the wrong rate when the plan is uncertain. If the trip might shrink to three days or stretch to three weeks, say so at the counter, because a good desk would rather structure the rental correctly once than unwind it twice. Uncertainty is a planning input, not an embarrassment.</p>
<p>Our team at Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu is happy to run your dates against all three rates for the electric motorbike, confirm availability, and hand over a machine that passes inspection. Plan the stay honestly, and the rate picks itself: 200,000 VND per day, 1,000,000 VND per week, or the monthly figure that makes a long stay cheap.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions about electric motorbike weekly rental prices in Hanoi</h2>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>How much does an electric motorbike cost per week to rent in Hanoi?</h2>
<p>The verified weekly rate is 1,000,000 VND for a full week, averaging roughly 143,000 VND per day. The same machine rents at 200,000 VND per day and 1,500,000 VND per month.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>How many days before the electric weekly rate beats the daily rate?</h2>
<p>Five days. Five daily rates total exactly 1,000,000 VND, matching the weekly rate, so any stay of five days or more should be booked weekly, saving 400,000 VND over a full seven days paid daily.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h2>Is the electric motorbike weekly price the same as the Honda Air Blade?</h2>
<p>Yes. The Honda Air Blade and Honda Vision rent at the same verified 1,000,000 VND per week alongside the electric. The entry tier, Honda Wave, Yamaha Sirius, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click, rents at 700,000 VND per week.</p>
</article>
